parts.text:
- Site 1 (province_19, province_3, province_40, province_9): all on island_4
- Site 2 (province_24, province_33, province_17, province_29): all on island_5

So the 2 eligible T1 sites are on different islands. This means both could be flipped (one per island). 

With the 50% skip chance, we get 4 combinations:
- skip both: original topology
- flip site 1 only: site 1 flipped
- flip site 2 only: site 2 flipped
- flip both: both flipped

4 distinct topologies. Matches the sweep result.

Now let me check one more potential issue: the test for "uses the seed to vary geometry and at least one land subdivision topology":

```javascript
it("uses the seed to vary geometry and at least one land subdivision topology",()=>{
 const a=result(0),b=result(1),c=result(42);
 expect(JSON.stringify(a.islands.map(i=>i.polygon))).not.toEqual(JSON.stringify(b.islands.map(i=>i.polygon)));
 expect(JSON.stringify(a.provinces.map(p=>p.polygon))).not.toEqual(JSON.stringify(b.provinces.map(p=>p.polygon)));
 const graph=(g:IslandGeography)=>JSON.stringify(g.provinces.map(p=>[p.id,[...p.borders].sort()]));
 expect(new Set([graph(a),graph(b),graph(c)]).size).toBeGreaterThan(1);
});
```

This requires that for seeds 0, 1, 42:
- Island polygons differ
- Province polygons differ
- Border graphs (topologies) are not all the same

Let me check what topologies seeds 0, 1, 42 have:

