parts.text:
Good. All types are exported.

Now let me also check whether the implementation handles the case where `corridorErrors` returns a non-empty array but the reservation is still acceptable. Looking at the code:

```javascript
for(const r of rng.shuffled(g.entranceReservations)){
 if(corridorErrors(g,r).length===0)continue;
 const original=r.points,choices=ports(r.from).flatMap(a=>ports(r.to).filter(b=>norm2(a,b)>=(20*Q)**2).map(b=>({a,b,cost:norm2(a,original[0]!)+norm2(b,original.at(-1)!)}))).sort((a,b)=>a.cost-b.cost);
 let accepted=false;
 for(const {a,b}of choices){r.points=[[...a],[...b]];if(corridorErrors(g,r).length===0){accepted=true;break;}}
 if(!accepted)r.points=original;
}
```

So if a reservation has no errors, skip. Otherwise, try alternatives. If no alternative works, restore original (with errors).

This is consistent with the contract: "failed candidates are never relabelled as successes". A reservation that fails all alternatives is restored to original (which also fails), and the candidate fails.

Now let me check one more time: when `repairReservations` restores the original, the candidate has errors. So `candidateErrors` will catch it. Good.

But wait - there's a subtle issue. If `corridorErrors(g,r)` returns errors but the original is restored, then `candidateErrors` will call `corridorErrors(g,r)` again and find errors. So the candidate fails. Good.

OK I think the implementation is correct.

Let me also verify the implementation doesn't have any clock-dependent behavior:

