12 edges are eligible for T1 flips (without considering border headroom constraint). The flip-probe found only 2 accepted flips. The difference is because:
- The flip-probe also checks that the flip produces valid board (T62 inspection).
- Some edges are eligible but produce invalid boards.

Hmm but my counting doesn't include the headroom check (borders+lanes<5). The implementation also includes this check:
```javascript
if(ps.slice(2).some(p=>p.borders.length>=4||p.borders.length+lanes(p.id)>=5)||rng.nextInt(0,1)===0)continue;
```

But this uses `p.borders.length`, which in the witness is not set (only the partial geography has it).

Wait, in the implementation:
```javascript
function flipSubdivisions(g:IslandGeography,rng:DeterministicRng):void {
 derive(g.provinces);
 // ...
 for(const e of rng.shuffled([...all.values()])){
  const {a,b,ids}=e;if(ids.length!==2||owners.get(key(a))!.length!==3||owners.get(key(b))!.length!==3)continue;
  // ...
  const ps=[...ids,r,s].map(id=>g.provinces.find(p=>p.id===id)!);
  if(changed.has(ps[0]!.island))continue;
  const lanes=(id:string)=>g.entranceReservations.filter(r=>r.from===id||r.to===id).length;
  if(ps.slice(2).some(p=>p.borders.length>=4||p.borders.length+lanes(p.id)>=5)||rng.nextInt(0,1)===0)continue;
```

`derive(g.provinces)` is called at the start, computing borders from polygons. Then `p.borders.length` is the count of borders for that province.

So the implementation uses the computed borders to check headroom.

In my count, I didn't apply the headroom check (because witness.provinces doesn't have borders in the JSON). Let me redo with computed borders:

